  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nWith 1.54\" PWATs observed from the KCRP 18Z sounding, there's not\nenough moisture for the seabreeze to do much of anything in terms\nof giving us an isolated storm... in fact, much of the Coastal\nBend remained cloud-free through 2 PM. But fear not humidity\nlovers, you're in store for another muggy next few days. PWATs are\nforecast to increase to 2.0-2.25\" through Thursday night. Combined\nwith the seabreeze and upper-level disturbances riding the\nsouthern periphery of the summer-long high pressure system, we'll\nhave better chances for rain chances.\n\nWhile models have been consistent with the moisture advection, it\nis worth noting that there still is some disagreement with how\nwidespread showers and thunderstorms will be, especially across\nthe Victoria Crossroads. While the NBM's PoP near 80-90%, looking\nat multi-run changes at Victoria show that while the rain is\nconsistently there (above 30% PoPs, global ensemble models are\nstruggling at how high PoPs get). For this reason, I've limited\nMax PoP on Thursday to be similar to the previous' package\nstrategy of limiting PoP to 65% along the coast and 55% further\ninland. As the 18Z CAM runs come in, this will help guide how \nmuch further PoP intervention will be needed on Thursday.\nUnfortunately, PoP becomes more so <35% closer to the Rio Grande.\nRain chances begin to drop off Friday as the PWATs come back down\nand South Texas is back again at the mercy of isolated seabreeze \nconvection.\n\nModels show a long easterly fetch developing across the northern \ngulf waters this week which may help push water farther up the \nbeaches. Will continue to monitor trends for slightly higher than \nnormal tides and/or an increased rip current risk.  At this time, \nminor coastal flooding is not anticipated but will continue to be \nmonitored.\n\nAnd of course it'll be hot for those locations that don't see any\nseabreeze storm Wednesday. We'll have to monitor heat index trends\ntonight to see if a Heat Advisory is needed Wednesday, but for\nnow, we continue to messaging of hydrate, hydrate, hydrate!",
